Z||000017||maryjane||09-14-2001||04:27 PM||||Tonker, I think you and a few others here are going to be in for a big surprise in the next few weeks-months.In war, nice guys finish last. The meek may inherit the Earth, but usually it is only a small plot, six feet deep. Do you really think that the extremist Islamics are going to wear a sign on their chest, "I am the enemy"? How are you going to distinguish between the good-evil? Have you not seen pictures of the children in that part of the world carrying weapons and using them? I've been thru this before, if you just assume that most people are innocent, those innocents oft times are the ones that you have to watch. They may not say it in public, but I would be willing to bet that the majority of the people in the Mideast would like to see us take it on the chin, and hard. Answer this question in NUMBERS please, before you respond to the rest of this post. How many Canadian soldiers and your friends and relatives are you willing to put at risk in order to keep from killing what may-or-not be non-combatants? Better educate yourself about what real combat is about before you start laying down 'rules of engagement'. This is not going to be pretty. Steel yourself for it. If your thinking surgical strikes, forget it. It's going to be down & dirty, no-holds-barred, blood, guts, and body bag combat. You say Canada is ready to do their part. I'm beginng to wonder if you really know what you're signing on for.||||reg||1
Z||000018||AkursedX||09-14-2001||04:43 PM||||Well, I will try to put this in some sort of perspective for people to understand a little better.  <P>The Muslim religion is about much more than terrorism and hating America.  It is a large, organized religion, just like Christianity.<P>Now, for the vast majority of Christians, we are fairly peaceful and respect other people.  But, we do have our radical groups.  Groups such as the KKK, that Arian Brotherhood etc..., now, these groups have committed acts of terrorism themselves, and of course, we have fought to be rid of them.<P>And the same thing goes for the Muslim religion.  These terrorist groups are the same types of radicals who are commiting these acts.  The vast majority of Muslims are just like the vast majority of Christians, fairly peaceful citizens.  So don't judge an entire religion based on a group of radicals.  It would be like jugding Christians based on KKK members.<P>On a sidenote:  I was in a discussion today with one of my professors, and an interesting idea came up.  Let's say we die and our spirits go wandering up to the sky.....and when we get up there, we find that Allah is really the one in charge.  And then the Christians are the one's who will be going to hell. while the Muslims are dancing in Heaven.  <BR>That's the funny thing about religion, there is no way to prove that your religion is the right one.  All you have is your faith, but what if your putting your faith in the wrong one??<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||Tonker||09-14-2001||04:49 PM||||MJ,<P>I'm fully prepared to accept civilian casualties. I am not prepared however, to accept genocide. It's absolutely wrong. It's as wrong, if not more so than the attack on the States. I would say this even if it were Canada that bore the brunt of the attack. It's easy to say this when it hasn't happened I know, but I like to think that I would continue to espouse this moral view it in the face of adversity.<P>How many Candians am I prepared to risk to do the right thing? As many as it takes to get the job done. That doesn't mean we go out there and do it foolishly. It means we do it judiciously.  I continue to have friends in the military and am considering re-enlisting in our regular force myself. I am prepared to put my life on the line to do the right thing. How many Canadians am I prepared to send over to do the wrong thing? None.<P>To be honest though, it's a moot point. It's something that won't be permitted by the international community or the UN. It's in clear violation of international law. I believe it is termed a crime against humanity. As evidenced by the war crimes trials of the Nazis and Slobo & Friends of the former Yugoslavia this sort of thing is not tolerated. And realistically, from what I've seen through news coverage, it's not an approach being considered by the US leadership.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||4
