Z||000031||maryjane||09-14-2001||11:18 PM||||I'm not arguing with anyone. The last couple of military involvements have been relativly clean. There are going to be hard choices to make, here at home, and in the battleline.Tonker, you skirted the question I asked. Reread the post. <P>A very likely scenario: You are a company commander in an Islamic country hunting down terrorists. They have the support of the people in the town/city they are hiding in. You and your men approach an apartment housing civilians and the suspected members of the terrrorist organization. You begin taking fire from the apts. You have casualties. You have a choice to make.<BR> <BR>Do you call in artillery/air strike and reduce the apts to rubble, thus killing/wounding the terrorists and any civilians present? You have probably just saved some of your own men's lives with this option.<P>Do you force your assualt, do a room by room search, probably taking more casualties in the process, to acheive your objective? (remember, you're the CC, you're the one that has to write the letter back to the dead soldier's families)<P>Do you call off the assualt, allowing the escape of the terrorists and their supporters, thus risking the same situation the next day in another apt, and yet more casualties of your own.<BR> <BR>Any Soldier/Marine will tell you the first responsibility is to YOUR men. <BR>Living thru a war is a matter of training and a lot of luck. Living with it for the rest of your life is a lot harder thing to bear. You will see your men's faces the rest of your life. That's the reason I posed the question to you Tonker. You would "risk no lives to do the wrong thing" is pretty dam* vague. You won't have the luxury of quoting international law in the middle of a firefight, or asking the UN to give a judment call, or asking for public opinion. It's all up to you. I don't doubt your resolve and admire your ethics and principles. Believe me, they all are what is moot when it's your men and friends that are lying there with their guts hanging out. Medals and commendations are usually awarded posthumusly. The best medal is a live man's smile.<BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||Cheever3000||09-14-2001||11:38 PM||||I've got a bone to pick, and Ed started just the right topic for me to post it on.  First, I could never carry out a voilent act upon an innocent Muslim or anyone with the appearance of third-world nationality, if they are in my country legally.  Just understand that I do not condone any of the attacks committed on these people as have been recently reported.  Second, I would LOVE for someone knowledgeable in such matters to correct me if my views are wrong.  Now...<P>What bothers me is how they whine & complain when some world event results in local hatred on them for things their people do.  Why does that bother me?  Because they don't seem to do ANYTHING about it.  In fact, what I typically hear from them is in <B>defense</B> of the terrorists, and criticism of America.  These last few days, they wouldn't dare do that for fear of a severe beating on the head with a flagpole, but when they aren't in such fear of reprisal, they have not shown evidence of being on "our side" against terrorism.  They routinely justify it.  So what do they expect from us now? They don't seem to be <I>against</I> the terrorists, so they must be <I>for</I> them. <P>Let me put it another way.  My ancestry is Irish.  If Irish terrorists were responsible for frequent attacks throughout the world on innocent people, I wouldn't be sitting on my duff expecting everybody else here in the U.S. to leave me alone and not pick on me.  I would be doing <I>something</I> to try to influence those in my homeland to do something about it!  I would be crying out very loud for action, making sure they understood what it means to my safety and quality of life here.  I would organize my fellow Irish-Americans to make public outcries against the terrorists who put us in such a situation.  I don't see this from third-world people in America.  <P>They seem to think "I leave you alone, so you leave me alone", but they <B>have</B> a responsibility to <B>help</B> us fight terrorism!  The perception is "guilty by association".  <P>About the Timothy McVeigh's and Klan Kooks, etc. -- at least we're resisting them.  The rest of us white people are not sitting around silently, <I>allowing</I> them to continue their hideous acts without consequence.<p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 09-14-2001).]||||reg||1
