Z||000035||Tonker||09-15-2001||01:46 AM||||MJ,<P>The Usual Niceties:<BR>First of all I have to disagree with one point. My first responsibility isn't to the men, it's to the mission. The mission, the men, then you. Everything else comes dead last. There are some cases where I would violate this rule, but you can't worry about losing lives at the expense of you objective, otherwise you might as well not be there at all.<P>The problem were having is that we're looking at this from two different levels. Rather than answer this question directly I'm going to tell you how I would handle things from both perspectives. Here is how I would assuage my bleeding heart conscience.<P>At the strategic level:<BR>1) Once it's determined who we are really looking for - let's be honest Bin-Ladin should be on the list regardless of whether he planned and organised this attack. He was found responsible for a previous attack and is an on-going security risk - and where the individuals are hiding, you tell the appropriate government that they will immediately hand his ass over or face the consequences of a war against the international coalition arrayed against them. If they hand him over, great, look at how many lives you've just saved.<P>2) If they refuse, you inform them that there now exists a state of war. The country will be informed that any opposition will be considered to be an enemy force. Any civillian center found harbouring the terrorist will also be considered to be an enemy force. All enemy forces will be eliminated. You've just given them the chance to save their own people. If they refuse, the blood is now on their hands.<P>3)The use of missiles and airstrikes on strategic targets can be used to indicate that we are serious.<P>4) The troops go in and start kicking ass. Where possible, the population should be informed to remain in their homes for the duration of hostilities to reduce unnecessary casualties.<P>Tactical:<BR>5) When my platoon and I get to this village, their government and the people have been informed about the consequences of harboring terrorists. I know that my government and superiors have taken every possible opportunity to to save innocent lives. That being said the lives of my men are my priority and responsibility. This is where I call in the air-strike and let God sort 'em out. This, of course, depends on my mission. If my superiors want this guy alive reducing the building to rubble isn't going to accomplish it, is it?<P>Let me point out that I do not begin with the assumption that all the "rag-heads" deserve to die. Where possible I try to avoid civillian casualties. Despite this I'm not going to allow my men to be slaughtered just to save these people. This is what I meant by proceeding judiciously. <P>The Real Issue:<BR>That fact remains that this thread is dedicated to the argument that Islamics are evil and deserve to die. Also that "America has always borne the burden of acting more " 'human' that(sic) other nations" and that Islamics should be exterminated "regardless of humanity restraints" because they "want to exterminate us". I argue that, this argument is absolutely without merit. It is based on the incorrect premise that Muslims as a whole want to destroy us. This is simply not the case. It is not part of their religion, it is not part of their nature. It is instead a small disenfranchised group no different than terrorist groups we have seen in western culture. The fact that this group invokes their religion in attempt to justify their actions does not mean that it is actually part of their religion. Christians rode into the Middle East in the dark ages, slaughtering Muslims and Jews (and each other)  in their zealousness to recover Jerusalem in the name of the Church. This is hardly indicative of 'typical' Christian values is it?<P>Also, at no point are we absolved of our requirement to act humanely. It is for this very reason that the Geneva Conventions were established. Humanity in warfare - as unlikely as it seems - is just as important as humanity in peace time. The fact the the Nazis and the Japanese violated the Geneva Conventions did not stop the Allies from treating German prisoners of war as was required by the Convention during WW2. Instead it steeled our resolve to punish those who violated these rules. Nuremburg was not about retribution or revenge, it was about teaching us about the costs of forgetting our humanity.<P>I have never  argued that we must avoid civilian casualties at all costs. I have argued that we have a responsibility to behave towards others as we would have others behave towards us if the situations were reversed. I don't say this because of any religious or cultural belief, but instead because I know it to be right. From what I've read others apparently know it to be right as well. I'm hoping the rest of the people are simply showing a natural human reaction to pain and/or fear. I want to find the people who caused this atrocious act, I want them to be punished in what ever manner is fitting and I want to make damn sure this never happens again. I don't believe that genocide is the way to do this. <P>MJ, I've tried to answer your question as openly and completely as I can. Now I have a question, not only for you but for everyone else who reads this. It is basically the distilled issue of this thread, as I understand it after having read Eds' initial post. It is a fairly straightforward one and one that people seem to keep skirting about. Is it acceptable to eradicate an entire race of people as Eds is suggesting?  Not is it acceptable to kill civilians or should the lives of our troops have priority or should we punish terrorists. I have presented my position and arguments already. <B>Is <I>genocide</I> acceptable to you, yes or no?</B><p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000036||Tonker||09-15-2001||01:59 AM||||BTW, MJ. Your question was excellent and caused me to think very hard about this. Also, you're absolutely right:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>The best medal is a live man's smile.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'd never heard it put that way, but I'm going to remember it forever.||||reg||14
Z||000037||maryjane||09-15-2001||02:31 AM||||In a word-NO. It was evil when Hitler did it, when Japan did it, when PolPot did it and in the Baltic nations.I think we're on the same page. I could be wrong, but if you could poll most of the Islamic people, I believe they would be happy to see the Jewish State exterminated, as well as the U.S. I do not feel the same way about them, and when we've all had time to cool down, a lot of those who are calling for all out war on all of the Mideast, will rethink what they are saying. But I don't think that THIS country will be satisfied with the Taliban just turning over Bin Laden now, it's gone far past that.<BR>Even if they did, another would take his place , and another, unless they had nowhere to operate from. Which brings us to the real point of this operation. It is not just to exact revenge, see justice done,(although that is part of it) or any of the other retorical things that are flying about. It is to try to stamp out this type of act in the future, or at least let it be known, by our actions, that a country harboring and abbetting them will pay a price for their role.. As I've previously stated, I doubt no one's courage or resolve.<BR>It's been a long time since NATO & it's allies have carried out a prolonged military<BR>campaign, possibly in several theaters at once. The younger generation has no idea of the possible (probable) consequences. I just want to try to jog a few memories that these things aren't going to be glorious, just heartbreaking. It's to the younger members that I'm trying to convey most of this. It's real easy to get mad and say, " I'm in, let me at em." A lot different once they are there. You've seen the terrain in Afganistan and other nations in the Mideast.<BR>We're going to take a lot of casualties in this I'm afraid. A lot more like Viet Nam or Korea than the Gulf. But I also believe, that once committed, we cannot be wimpy about it.<BR>I'm all for this war, I just want us all to have our eyes open going in.<BR>To exemplify what I'm saying about this being a lot more than a few cruise missles and a SEAL team or two, War Bonds will start being sold in this country within the next two weeks for the first time since WWII.<BR>Have a good weekend. ||||reg||1
